le to buy insurance  but it's gonna be with such a high  deductible that it's not going to be  very useful to them so the you know  there was one big change that I think  was important which is the House bill  took out pre-existing conditions  protections in a really big way this  does it in a sort of more subtle way  which we could talk about but I think if  if President Trump was worried about  that about pre-existing conditions then  maybe he is satisfied that this bill is  a little bit less mean but in terms of  lots of people and particularly  vulnerable people who need health care  losing their insurance I think this bill  has some of the same effects as the  House version according to the  Congressional Budget Office the largest  savings in this bill comes from  reductions in Medicaid spending what  effectively did would that  I think it could have some pretty  substantial effect so the thing to  understand is that there's kind of two  parts of Medicaid there was the Medicaid  that existed before the Affordable Care  Act and then there was an expansion to  Medicaid that happened as part of the  bill so part of the Affordable Care Act  was that Medicaid went to a lot of low  income adults who didn't have children  who didn't have good health insurance  options before but the changes under  this bill would affect the entire  program so that includes children the  elderly disabled Americans and some very  poor parents of young children or  pregnant women and so you know the  assumption is that states will be able  to make up the difference or states  would be able to find more efficient  ways to provide care for those  populations but if you listen to the  states they say it's really not possible  that this really just amounts to a  budget cuts and with the total result  and cutbacks in care  you mentioned pre-existing conditions  let's talk about that what it what is  the measurable difference between the  two bills and what does it mean for  Americans that have pre-existing  conditions so what the Affordable Care  Act said is that if you want to buy  insurance you get to buy it at the same  price of anyone else regardless of  whether you have had an illness in your  past and the House bill said no insurers  should be allowed to sell insurance to  people who have a history of illness at  a higher price than people who have been  healthy so that's not part of this bill  this bill does not it's it still says  like Obamacare that everyone has to be  charged the same price but it allows  states to get rid of rules that set  minimum benefit standards so Obamacare  says if you want to sell health  insurance it has to include prescription  drug coverage it has to include hospital  care it has to include maternity  benefits and this bill would allow  states to say no no it doesn't have to  cover those things so you could imagine  that someone say who has cancer might be  able to buy a plan at a good price in a  state that gets rid of these rules but  that plan might not actually cover all  of the care that they need so it's not  as bad for people with pre-existing  conditions but it could be worse for  people who are sick okay an important  difference there yeah and we still have  no final legislation the other board no  more Grossinger cats thank you so much  for being with us this morning  thanks a lot
 

